From 90e7d71ca8f05ce3077c85670e557d095238b596 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gianluca Mazza <info@gianlucamazza.it>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 00:23:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] drm/msm/dsi: derive v2 link clocks from the rate the PLL
 generates

On DSI v2 (MSM8960/APQ8064) the byte, source and pixel clocks all come
from the DSI PLL. The pixel RCG cannot change the rate of its parent and
only accepts a parent that matches the requested pixel clock times one
of its fixed fractions within 100 kHz.

dsi_calc_clk_rate_v2() derived all rates from the mode clock. When the
PLL cannot generate the resulting byte clock exactly, the source clock
ends up at the generated rate while the pixel clock is still requested
at the ideal rate, and clk_set_rate() on the pixel clock fails:

  dsi_link_clk_set_rate_v2: Failed to set rate pixel clk, -22

This happens on the Nexus 7 (2013): the JDI LT070ME05000 mode needs a
byte clock of 116.61975 MHz, the PLL generates 116.5 MHz, and the panel
stays dark.

Round the byte clock with clk_round_rate() and, if it differs, derive
the source and pixel clocks from the rounded rate. The pixel clock then
becomes 155.333 MHz, the source clock 466 MHz, and the pixel RCG uses
its 1/3 fraction.

The pixel clock is rounded up. The PLL output divider that feeds the
pixel RCG cannot change the VCO rate and rounds its divider up, so a
pixel rate truncated to 155333333 Hz asks for a 465999999 Hz parent,
selects divider 3 instead of 2 and yields 310666667 Hz, which the pixel
RCG rejects again with -EINVAL.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5-2
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
index 982abaa..2b4ec53 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -685,11 +685,34 @@ int dsi_calc_clk_rate_v2(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host, bool is_bonded_dsi)
 	u32 bpp = mipi_dsi_pixel_format_to_bpp(msm_host->format);
 	unsigned int esc_mhz, esc_div;
 	unsigned long byte_mhz;
+	long byte_rate;
 
 	dsi_calc_pclk(msm_host, is_bonded_dsi);
 
 	msm_host->src_clk_rate = mult_frac(msm_host->pixel_clk_rate, bpp, 8);
 
+	/*
+	 * The byte, source and pixel clocks are all generated by the DSI PLL,
+	 * and the pixel RCG cannot change the rate of its parent. If the PLL
+	 * cannot generate the requested byte clock exactly, derive the source
+	 * and pixel clocks from the rate it does generate, so that the pixel
+	 * RCG can find a matching divider.
+	 *
+	 * Round the pixel clock up: the PLL output divider feeding the pixel
+	 * RCG rounds its divider up, so a pixel rate truncated below the exact
+	 * fraction of the source clock would select the next larger divider.
+	 */
+	byte_rate = clk_round_rate(msm_host->byte_clk, msm_host->byte_clk_rate);
+	if (byte_rate > 0 && byte_rate != msm_host->byte_clk_rate) {
+		msm_host->byte_clk_rate = byte_rate;
+		msm_host->src_clk_rate = byte_rate * msm_host->lanes;
+		msm_host->pixel_clk_rate =
+			DIV_ROUND_UP_ULL((u64)byte_rate * 8 * msm_host->lanes,
+					 bpp);
+		DBG("rounded pclk=%lu, bclk=%lu", msm_host->pixel_clk_rate,
+		    msm_host->byte_clk_rate);
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * esc clock is byte clock followed by a 4 bit divider,
 	 * we need to find an escape clock frequency within the
-- 
